  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMore rounds of showers and storms are expected through the \nremainder of the afternoon into the early evening. With the \nmoisture rich environment, these storms have been efficient \nheavy rainfall producers, and a localized flash flooding \npotential remains a concern. The area of focus for the heavy \nrainfall and flash flooding potential remains near the foothills\nand Lakes Regions of Maine and New Hampshire, parts of which \nalso saw some heavy rainfall earlier this morning.\n\nThese showers and storms are expected to diminish through the \nevening hours, with a weak front slipping through during the \novernight. This front begins the process of gradually bringing a\ndrier airmass back into New England.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSunday looks like another very warm day with highs pushing into\nthe upper 80s to near 90 downwind of the mountains. Dew points \nlook to mainly be in the 60s during the daytime, keeping heat \nindices mainly in the low 90s to near the mid 90s. So while the \ncoast will flirt with heat indices near 95 degrees tomorrow, a \nheat advisory doesn't appear warranted at this point.\n\nSome scattered pop up showers and storms will still be possible\ntomorrow, especially across the north, but do not look as \nwidespread as today's activity.\n\nWith the return of some relatively drier air, low temps fall \nmore Sunday night than we've seen for the last few nights. \nWidespread lows in the low to mid 60s look likely, with upper \n50s across the north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMonday will remain warm to hot with highs well into the 80s to\naround 90. However, dew point temperatures will be noticeably\nlower making for lower heat indices and a drier feel.\n\nSeveral members of the guidance suite are in agreement that a\nshort wave trough will move across the forecast area during the\nevening hours along with an accompanying SFC cold front. \nChances for showers and thunderstorms will increase during the \nlate afternoon and evening hours with a chance that a few could\nproduce strong winds, with DCAPE approaching 1000 Joules in \nspots, although SBCAPE seems to be somewhat limited at this \ntime.\n\nA series of additional cold fronts will cross through mid to \nlate week, bringing rounds of scattered showers and possible \nthunderstorms... although no day currently looks like a washout.\nTemperatures will also cool towards seasonable averages with \nhighs into the upper 70s to lower 80s. Dew points will lower as \nwell with overnight low temperatures into the 50s.",
